Q: THTTPRio Authorization Bearer A: <p>You can use the <code>THTTPRIO.HTTPWebNode.OnBeforePost</code> event to customize <code>THTTPRIO</code>'s HTTP requests.</p>
<p>In 10.2 and earlier, the event's <code>Data</code> parameter is a raw <code>Pointer</code>. By default, <code>THTTPRIO</code> uses the WinInet API, in which case the <code>Data</code> will be a WinInet <code>HINTERNET</code> request handle that you can pass to any WinInet request function, such as <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/wininet/nf-wininet-httpaddrequestheadersw" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>HttpAddRequestHeaders()</code></a>, eg:</p>
<pre class="lang-pascal prettyprint-override"><code>uses
  ..., Winapi.WinInet;

procedure TMyForm.HTTPRIO1HTTPWebNode1BeforePost(const HTTPReqResp: THTTPReqResp;
  Data: Pointer);
var
  Token: string;
  AuthHdr: string;
begin
  Token := ...;
  AuthHdr := 'Authorization: Bearer ' + Token + #13#10;
  HttpAddRequestHeaders(Data, PChar(AuthHdr), Length(AuthHdr), HTTP_ADDREQ_FLAG_ADD);
end;
</code></pre>
<p>There is also an option to have <code>THTTPRIO</code> use Indy instead of WinInet (by re-compiling the <code>Soap.SOAPHTTPTrans</code> unit with <code>USE_INDY</code> defined), in which case the event's <code>Data</code> parameter will be a pointer to an Indy <code>TIdHTTP</code> object, and you can use its <code>Request.CustomHeaders</code> property, eg:</p>
<pre class="lang-pascal prettyprint-override"><code>uses
  ..., IdHTTP;

procedure TMyForm.HTTPRIO1HTTPWebNode1BeforePost(const HTTPReqResp: THTTPReqResp;
  Data: Pointer);
var
  Token: string;
begin
  Token := ...;
  with TIdHTTP(Data).Request do
  begin
    BasicAuthentication := False; // &lt;- important to set when using custom authentication...
    CustomHeaders.Values['Authorization'] := 'Bearer ' + Token;
  end;
end;
</code></pre>
<hr />
<p>That being said, in 10.3 and later, <code>THTTPRIO</code> uses Embarcadero's own HTTP framework exclusively, and so the <code>Data</code> parameter has been replaced with a <code>THTTPClient</code> parameter, which has its own <code>CustomHeaders</code> property, eg:</p>
<pre class="lang-pascal prettyprint-override"><code>procedure TMyForm.HTTPRIO1HTTPWebNode1BeforePost(const HTTPReqResp: THTTPReqResp;
  Client: THTTPClient);
var
  Token: string
begin
  Token := ...;
  Client.CustomHeaders['Authorization'] := 'Bearer ' + Token;
end;
</code></pre>

Q: Why does Python not call the function descriptor inside this other descriptor? A: <p>The problem is simply that in your snippet, the decorated method is assigned as the descriptor <code>value</code> attribute, but when <code>__get__</code> is called, it is returned unchanged.
If you want it to be bound to the owner instance, you have to do that yourself.  If your goal is to decorate functions this way, you'd better just leave <code>__set__</code> without any implementation, so that you have a <code>non data</code> descriptor (just like regular methods behave in Python):</p>
<pre><code>from functools import partial


class Identity:
    def __init__(self, value):
        self.value = value

    def __get__(self, obj, objtype=None):
        if obj is None:
            return self
        # returns a partial instance, which is functionally equivalent to a 
        # native &quot;bound method&quot;
        return partial(self.value, obj)
        # alternatively, you can use the built-in descriptor mechanism
        # of Python functions itself for a  &quot;real&quot; bound method:
        return self.value.__get__(obj, objttype)


    def __repr__(self):
        return f&quot;Descriptor Identity, wrapping {self.value}&quot;

class Example:
    @Identity
    def non_method():
        pass



</code></pre>
<p>(On a side note: when experimenting with these mechanisms, and trying to investigate what is called and what is not, I recommend just writing in some side effects into the code you are not sure is running itself: in other words, add some <code>print</code> calls into the <code>__get__</code> (and when you get to it) the <code>__set__</code> and <code>__set_name__</code> methods: it will make your understanding much easier)</p>
<p>Your code works the &quot;decorator&quot; side of it ok - but it is good to keep in mind that the decorator behavior is not inherent to a descriptor.
So, just to recap that:
People frequently  mistake what the descriptor protocol does, with the features that the built-in <code>property</code> provides.</p>
<p>While <code>property</code> does indeed works like you try to do there, it does so <em>not</em> because it is a descriptor, but because it is coded to work like a decorator, and then wiring up its own descriptor &quot;dunder&quot; methods to work through the methods that were decorated.</p>
<p>The descriptor protocol states that if an instance (of, say, class <code>Identity</code>) that is the value of a <em>class</em> (say, of class <code>Example</code>) attribute features any of the <code>__get__</code>, <code>__set__</code> or <code>__delete__</code> methods it will work as a descriptor: instances of class <code>Example</code> will go through the descriptor methods for reading, writing or deleting the attribute, and accessing the attribute of the class <code>Example</code> itself will work even when reading the attribute from the <code>Example</code> class.</p>
<p>The <em>decorator</em>  is a separate thing: it is a syntax that calls the decorator callable with the class or function that is decorated as first positional parameter, and replaces that function (or class) in the same namespace with whatever the decorator callable returns.</p>
<p><code>property</code> creates a class that do both things, which you don't do in your <code>Identity</code> class. If you are to use a class as a decorator, the second argument (after <code>self</code> passed to the class <code>__init__</code> is the decorated method. The instance of <code>Identity</code> itself then replaces that method in the class body.</p>
